Joe,
It is hard to answer your question because you are telling us where you want to end up but not where you are starting from.

If you have your raw data in machine readable form or in your workspace already, you can use cov(myData) to obtain the covariance matrix.

If you do not have your raw data but have a covariance matrix in machine readable form, you can read it in as a text file.

If you do not have either in a machine readable form, then your need to type the data by hand, it is just a matter of where you prefer to type it.

require(lavaan)
# Some data to work with
bollenData <- PoliticalDemocracy
head(bollenData)

# Compute a covariance matrix from data
bollenCov <- cov(bollenData)
round(bollenCov, 2)

# read and write raw data
write.csv(bollenData, 'Bollen Data.csv')
bollenDataCopy <- read.csv('Bollen Data.csv')
head(bollenDataCopy)

# read and write covariance matrix
write.csv(bollenCov, 'Bollen Covariance Matrix.csv')
bollenCovCopy <- read.csv('Bollen Covariance Matrix.csv')
bollenCovCopyMatrix <- as.matrix(bollenCovCopy[,2:12]) # eliminate row names read as first variable
rownames(bollenCovCopyMatrix) <- colnames(bollenCovCopyMatrix) # restore row names as row names
round(bollenCovCopyMatrix, 2)

# Not identical but very close
round(bollenCov - bollenCovCopyMatrix, 13)

Further details on data import and export can be found here:

https://cran.r-project.org/doc/manuals/R-data.html

Keith
