Look at varTable(fit) and you will see that one (or more) of your variables have very large variances relative to other variables. Standardizing this particular variable(s), if it makes sense, would make this message go away. Variables with very widely different variances can be problematic in SEM and path analysis.

> 2: In lavaan::lavaan(model = Across1, data = SEMtest, missing = "FIML",  :
>   lavaan WARNING: syntax contains parameters involving exogenous covariates; switching to fixed.x = FALSE
> This second error I am less clear on the meaning.
>

This is because of this line of code:

# residual correlations
ALD.vvi~~CanCov.vvi

ALD.vvi and CanCov.vvi are both covariates and not dependent variables in your model. Therefore, they are considered fixed and not random variables. When you estimate this covariance, they are now considered random and that's why lavaan is giving you that message. Given your comment that says "residual correlations", I wonder if you meant to have something else covary?

For coding indirect effects, this tutorial should help: http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/mediation.html

Best,
Chris

